{"tool_name":"chromnet","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"chromnet","home":"http://chromnet.cs.washington.edu/","summary":"Displays the nodes and edges of the chromatin network using a real-time force model.","addition_date":"2018-08-13T11:52:54Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:28:36.414307Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:chromnet","credit":[{"email":"suinlee@uw.edu","name":"Su-In Lee","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://chromnet.cs.washington.edu/#/"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Expression data visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0571"},{"term":"Peak calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3222"}]}],"name":"ChromNet","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"d.gabrielaitis","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s13059-016-0925-0","metadata":{"abstract":"A cell's epigenome arises from interactions among regulatory factors-transcription factors and histone modifications-co-localized at particular genomic regions. We developed a novel statistical method, ChromNet, to infer a network of these interactions, the chromatin network, by inferring conditional-dependence relationships among a large number of ChIP-seq data sets. We applied ChromNet to all available 1451 ChIP-seq data sets from the ENCODE Project, and showed that ChromNet revealed previously known physical interactions better than alternative approaches. We experimentally validated one of the previously unreported interactions, MYC-HCFC1. An interactive visualization tool is available at http://chromnet.cs.washington.edu.","authors":[{"name":"Hoffman M.M."},{"name":"Lee S.-I."},{"name":"Lundberg S.M."},{"name":"Penn L.Z."},{"name":"Raught B."},{"name":"Tu W.B."}],"citationCount":25,"date":"2016-04-30T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Biology","title":"ChromNet: Learning the human chromatin n